6. Conveying Non-Source Forms.

You may convey a covered work in object code form under the terms
of sections 4 and 5, provided that you also convey the
machine-readable Corresponding Source under the terms of this License,
in one of these ways:

a) Convey the object code in, or embedded in, a physical product
(including a physical distribution medium), accompanied by a
written offer, valid for at least three years and valid for as
long as you offer spare parts or customer service for that product,
to give anyone who possesses the object code either (1) a copy of
the Corresponding Source for all the software in the product on a
durable physical medium customarily used in the software industry,
or (2) access to download the Corresponding Source from a network
server at no charge.

b) Convey the object code in, or embedded in, a physical product
(including a physical distribution medium), accompanied by a
written offer, valid for at least three years and valid for as
long as you offer spare parts or customer service for that product,
to give anyone who possesses the object code either (1) a copy of
the Corresponding Source for all the software in the product on a
durable physical medium customarily used in the software industry,
or (2) access to download the Corresponding Source from a network
server at no charge.

c) Convey individual copies of the object code with a copy of the
Corresponding Source on a durable physical medium customarily used
in the software industry (for example, a CD-ROM), and include a
written offer, valid for at least three years, to provide the
Corresponding Source on a durable physical medium customarily used
in the software industry.

d) Convey the object code by offering access to download it from a
network server, and offer to provide the Corresponding Source via
download from the same network server at no charge. The
Corresponding Source must be available for at least as long as the
object code is being offered.

e) Convey the object code using peer-to-peer networks, provided
you inform other peers where the Corresponding Source and object
code are available.

A separable portion of the object code, whose source code is
Corresponding Source, may be excluded from the Corresponding Source
if it (1) is not part of the Corresponding Source for the work, and
(2) is excluded under the same conditions that would allow it to be
excluded from the Corresponding Source under section 3. A
separable portion of the object code, whose source code is not
Corresponding Source, need not be included in the Corresponding
Source.

